00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,560
The story of the value discrepancy at the core

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:06,434
of CENTS is told in the traits of Cent #1264.

3
00:00:07,546 --> 00:00:09,359
The starting point for Rutherford Chang,

4
00:00:09,439 --> 00:00:11,119
the fact that the copper in one penny

5
00:00:11,199 --> 00:00:13,679
was worth about 3 times as much as a penny,

6
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:16,000
has roots and implications that are activated

7
00:00:16,039 --> 00:00:18,422
by the system that he created with CENTS.

8
00:00:18,663 --> 00:00:22,943
Cent #1264 was struck in 1944,

9
00:00:22,983 --> 00:00:25,463
the year the postwar money system was built.

10
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:29,381
In July 1944, at Bretton Woods,

11
00:00:29,460 --> 00:00:32,021
44 nations tied their currencies to the US

12
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:33,941
dollar, and the dollar to gold,

13
00:00:34,021 --> 00:00:35,780
at 35 dollars an ounce.

14
00:00:35,860 --> 00:00:37,720
Gold was the measure of money.

15
00:00:38,326 --> 00:00:41,445
Cent #1264 is the oldest Cent in the collection

16
00:00:41,485 --> 00:00:43,779
with a gold trait, by 12 years.

17
00:00:44,606 --> 00:00:47,646
On August 15, 1971, the United States

18
00:00:47,805 --> 00:00:49,595
stopped exchanging dollars for gold.

19
00:00:50,526 --> 00:00:52,106
"Your dollar will be worth just

20
00:00:52,165 --> 00:00:53,765
as much tomorrow as it is today."

21
00:00:54,655 --> 00:00:56,442
Within two years the Bretton Woods system

22
00:00:56,521 --> 00:00:57,962
had collapsed, and currencies

23
00:00:58,042 --> 00:00:59,402
floated against each other.

24
00:00:59,481 --> 00:01:01,481
US currency became fiat:

25
00:01:01,561 --> 00:01:03,624
worth what it is trusted to be worth.

26
00:01:04,682 --> 00:01:06,641
Significant Obsidian is covering Lincoln's

27
00:01:06,721 --> 00:01:08,641
face: in the Atlas's words,

28
00:01:08,721 --> 00:01:10,641
a major visual actor with strong

29
00:01:10,721 --> 00:01:12,965
darkening, but not yet fully dominant.

30
00:01:13,575 --> 00:01:15,281
The unique form of the obsidian on this

31
00:01:15,361 --> 00:01:18,069
Cent gives it its nickname: the Alien Cent.

32
00:01:18,902 --> 00:01:21,894
This is an object that has become alien to itself.

33
00:01:23,102 --> 00:01:24,421
Dislocating the global reserve

34
00:01:24,501 --> 00:01:26,501
currency from any material substance

35
00:01:26,581 --> 00:01:27,951
had wide-ranging effects,

36
00:01:29,182 --> 00:01:30,621
not only on wealth distribution

37
00:01:30,701 --> 00:01:31,637
and social equity,

38
00:01:32,594 --> 00:01:34,293
but on materiality itself.

39
00:01:35,248 --> 00:01:37,061
These effects continue to bear influence

40
00:01:37,102 --> 00:01:38,662
to this day, in economics

41
00:01:38,742 --> 00:01:39,679
as well as art.

42
00:01:40,915 --> 00:01:42,341
In the words of Adina Glickstein,

43
00:01:42,382 --> 00:01:44,542
in her article "Never Midcurve" for Spike

44
00:01:44,662 --> 00:01:46,061
Magazine: "Post-Bretton

45
00:01:46,102 --> 00:01:48,114
Woods as much as post-internet,

46
00:01:48,168 --> 00:01:49,861
hasn't contemporary art become,

47
00:01:49,902 --> 00:01:51,929
if not exactly a commercial product,

48
00:01:51,954 --> 00:01:54,245
